<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balkh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bactra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Balkh, known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as Bactra, is an ancient city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, near Mazar-i-Sharif, once the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>. It was a centre of Achaemenid, Greco-Bactrian, Kushan and early Islamic rule and a hub of Buddhist and Zoroastrian learning, later called the 'mother of cities'. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> sacked it in 1220, and it never recovered its former size.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balkh.jpg" alt="Balkh" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AhmadElhan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Green_Mosque,_Balkh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
